<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lyuh Woon-hyung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Yo Un-hyong, Yeo Un-hyeong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lyuh Woon-hyung was a Korean independence activist and moderate leftist politician. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in August 1945 he formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-for-the-preparation-of-korean-independence">Committee for the Preparation of Korean Independence</a>, which proclaimed the People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a> in September, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan">American occupation</a> refused to recognise it. He then worked for cooperation between left and right until he was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lyuh-woon-hyung.jpg" alt="Lyuh Woon-hyung" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuh_Woon-Hyung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lyuh Woon-hyung, known by the art name Mongyang, was born on 25 May 1886 in Yangpyeong, Gyeonggi Province, into the Hamyang Yeo clan. In 1907 he joined the National Debt Repayment Movement, and around then he became a Protestant and an associate of the American missionary Charles Allen Clark. In 1910 he broke with tradition by freeing the slaves of his household. He entered the Pyongyang Presbyterian Theological Seminary in 1911, and in 1914 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where he studied English literature at a university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> before moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> he founded in 1918 what became the Shanghai Korean People's Association and led the New Korean Youth League. In 1919 he took part in drawing up the February 8 Declaration of Independence in Tokyo, a precursor of the declaration that opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a>, and in efforts to send Korean delegates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>. In April 1919 he was among the founders of the Provisional Government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a>.</p><p>In 1920 he joined the Korean Communist Party. In 1922 he attended the Congress of the Peoples of the East in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a>. The Japanese authorities arrested him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1929 and he served three years in prison. From 1933 he headed the newspaper Chosŏn Chungang Ilbo, and was forced out in 1936 after Korean newspapers erased the Japanese flag from pictures of the Olympic gold medallist Sohn Kee-chung. In 1944, expecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s defeat, he organised a secret committee that spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Just before the surrender the Japanese official Endo Ryusaku made contact with him and agreed to release prisoners. The preparatory committee he founded on 17 August 1945 set up over 140 branches in north and south within the month. In October, under pressure from the United States Military Government, he stepped down from the People's Republic and formed the People's Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. When a movement to unite left and right arose in May 1946 he represented the centre-left, and was attacked from both extremes.</p><p>On 19 July 1947 he was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> by Han Chi-geun, a 19-year-old who had fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and belonged to the White Shirts Society, a right-wing terrorist group. His death was widely mourned. He is one of the few modern Korean politicians revered in both South and North Korea, and on 21 February 2008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> awarded him the Order of Merit for National Foundation posthumously.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lyuh Woon-hyung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On Japan's surrender the moderate leftist <b>Lyuh Woon-hyung</b> formed the Committee for the Preparation of Korean Independence, which organised local committees to keep order.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-republic-of-korea">People's Republic of Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yangpyeong County, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.49,127.49&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Yangpyeong County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7737 815 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lyuh Woon-hyung"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8187 1085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8187 1085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.49,127.49&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
